hen they become unhappy paranoid  clinging for the life of their egos to  their own self approval and blaming the  rest of the world for not endorsing it  this whole essay Springs from what I've  been telling you about my family and  what I'm going to tell you about them in  the light of the earnest and scientific  approach that all young people of your  generation bring to the task of bearing  and breeding and educating your young my  family and many families of that day  indeed many families of today seem like  irresponsible maniacs but all the  science in the world is no good without  this thing I have quickly and carelessly  called love there must be love  unselfconscious spontaneous and  unscientific shining out of all the  dusty corners of the disorderly  household and specifically the little  Adam whirling around among billions of  other atoms very like himself must be  given the illusion that they are not so  like himself that he is something very  special worth promoting worth perfecting  worth building up to that position of  prominence and achievement where he can  lie in his bed  or stand on a hill or walk down the  street and say to himself with  conviction what a good boy am I  the artist NC wats enthusiasm for the  world around him was apparent and nearly  all he did he encouraged his five gifted  children to stretch their imaginations  and creativity to the fullest three of  his children on react Caroline and  Andrew became artists his daughter and a  composer and painter and son at an  engineer and inventor Wyeth was an  exceptional letter writer who guided and  challenged his children and made certain  that life for them was full of wonder  imagination principle and productivity  we will end the evening tonight with a  letter from NC Wyeth to his son the  brilliant painter Andrew Wyeth reading  the letter will be Andrew Wyeth son NC  Wyeth grandson jamie wyeth it is  especially fitting in a hopeful  conclusion to have mr. Wyeth read as he  is a third generation of American  greatness and a painter who through his  work has heeded his grandfather's advice  I'm honored to present my friend jamie  wyeth  I thought I'd preface this letter to  describe this set this thing up a bit  with me I was born a year before my  grandfather was killed at age 60 and so  I never knew him growing up though his  studio was physically higher in the hill  above our family's house and i would  spend endless afternoon's going through  this incredible studio full of costumes  and cutlasses from Treasure Island and  blunderbusses for various books and so  forth and and then I would go in the  back of the studio and there were stacks  of his illustrations from all the  wonderful books that still have  resonance today with readers and so  forth them and I wanted to bring up the  resonance and particularly illustrates a  resonance I a few years ago the New York  Public Library which has the collection  of all his Robin Hood series and  Stephenson's kidnapped and they just had  restored them put them back into better  shape and so they were having sort of a  an opening to reap resent the paintings  and they invited me to come up I was 25  at the time I think her 30 and so they  were having his nice party and whatnot  and I was standing there and I felt this  sort of tug in my arm and I looked down  and here was this little old lady she  must have been I don't lady is there  early 90s and I said hello and she said  oh mr. Wyeth I am so thrilled to meet  you as a child I grew up with your  illustrations  either thought the Fountain of Youth was  in Chadds Ford but anyway this letter is  from written in his studio in Chadds  Ford Pennsylvania februari 16th 1944  dear andy there's a slow bill we win  coming down the valley comes in great  round intermittent puffs and piles up  about the studio ever so like some  engulfing and visible surf one can  almost hear it pouring off the back roof  cascades a spoon spending itself in  foaming at ease among the orchard trees  and tangled grass the day is somber and  gray and I'm a reminded of throw who  found the drab days of winter so  inspiring these dreary winter colors  which depressed other people suggested  to him the highest spiritual traits that  constituted his concept of beauty the  week has been to me a singular mix of  ineffable sadness and inspiration to  moods that often happen together but  there is a persistent melancholy which  I'm unable to shake off to circumvent  these feelings I've devoted most of my  spare time to reading especially at  night when sleep eludes me throw Goethe  Emerson Tolstoy all had struck me as  always with incisive vitality and  freshness my ruminations have again been  vividly stirred these great men forever  radiate a sharp sense of that profound  requirement of the artist to fully  understand that consequences of what he  creates are unimportant let the motive  for action be in the action itself not  in the event I know from my own  experience that when I create with any  degree of strength and beauty I have no  thought of consequences anyone who  creates for effect to score a hit does  not know what he is missing this period  of unprecedented distractions and over  stimulation constitutes a fierce  antagonist to the compliments  compliments of the spirit whatever is  worth discovering one's heart and mind  can only rise as a surface among quiet  conditions which one thought grows  besides another and one has time to  reflect periods of bleak thinking and  all steer feeling that kind of cuts to  the bone are imperative spiritual  experiences which so often masquerade  for cultural influences are so often  merely cozy and sociable I was struck  with a quotation from Michelangelo it is  only well with me when I have chisel in  my hand how stimulating is a company of  generous minds I'm thinking of the four  masters named above who overlooked  rifles and keep their minds  instinctively fixed on whatever is good  and positive in the world about them  true magnanimous people have no vanity  no jealousy they have no reserves  they're fed on the true and solid  wherever they find it what is more they  find it everywhere there's little doubt  that the modern line is opposed to the  romantic mind the modern mine is mainly  content to ask and seek causes and  consequences whereas a romantic mine  seeks the significance of things the  romantic mind must be restored to his  necessary place of leadership if things  have no significance things are Hollow  the greats in all the arts have been  primarily romanticists in realest the  two cannot be separated they interpreted  life as they saw it but through every  lines being soaked in the consciousness  of the object when is bound to feel  beside life as it is the life that ought  to be and is that the captivates us all  great painting is something that  enriches and enhances life something  that makes it higher wider and deeper a  great painter is a great man painting  sound feeling can only exist in a man  who is living on all sides of life that  is natural damn an only through this  experience can he sense his times and  avoid that ever lurking pit full of  egocentrism someone uncommon ly wise  said nothing is so poor in melancholia  as art that is interested in itself and  not in its subject to live to keep one's  eyes open and wonder to be surprised by  things here's a quote which I think will  interest you great painting like box  music and texture closely woven subdued  like the early go blend tapestries no  emphasis  climax is no beginnings or endings  merely resumption xand transitions it  designs so sustain that there is no  effort in the starting and every casual  statement is equally great but of course  such depth presupposes another mode of  feeling one has to be a Bach before one  can paint in his power and richness that  the style can only spring from a Deeping  of our emotional life that is what we  really demand and look for there's a  real task on our hands Andy modern art  critics and their supine followers like  the flat and the shower they like it as  they like soft drinks and factory made  bread intensity distinction fire those  elements of mature sincerity these they  love they fear disturbance miles very  well we all are remember me to everybody  warmly and give little Nicholas a  special pad for me he then adds a PS  Monday 730am then reading this over I'm  impressed by the fact that none of the  thoughts expressed or new to you we have  together gone over these matters again  and again but as good I think to repeat  fundamental truths and if possible bring  them into new and fresh focus a great  truth is like a mountain that one walks  around and the changes of its contours  one moves his position only emphasize  Andhra vivify its majesty  thank you all so much for coming and  thank you all for your wonderful  readings thanks  posterity is dori mccullough Lawson's  first book for more information visit  the publishers website at Random House  calm / doubleday
 

